Z||000004||Fierowrecker||06-23-2001||01:57 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by KRMFiero:<BR><B> Thats pretty much what I figured, I'll have to look for a place to do it (Trans is fine just want it rebuilt just cause. Anyone know any places that rebuilds Trannys for a resonable price (I supply parts) In RI</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Hey KRM!<BR>I admire your currage!<BR>Do you have a service manual for the automatic in question?<BR>Are you good with puzzles??<BR>IT CAN BE DONE!!!<BR>I did it, so can you, if you take your time and lots of pictures!!!<BR>crash...<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||rockcrawl||06-23-2001||07:52 AM||||I've done two 4spd autos. Considering the shop wanted $900 to do my Ford AOD, I think it was worth doing it myself. It took 6 hours and $150 in parts. I was surprised how easy it really was, and I got by without any special tools. I'm sure I'll get flamed for suggesting that it's easy, but that's my opinion. I am a full time mechanic though. If you decide to do it, make sure you buy a good manual and have a HUGE clean workspace, preferably a steel top table or something like that. I used a door on a pair of sawhorses and a 1" deep fabricated steel pan about the same size as the door.<BR> Have fun and good luck!||||reg||1
Z||000006||SteveJ||06-23-2001||10:00 AM||||One of the advantages of our cars being so old is that replacement parts are cheap in the junk yards. If what you have there is a daily driver I'd say just go get another transmission. Should cost less than $250. I paid $225.<P>But considering you already bought the parts I guess you are set on your course. I'd just say relax and have fun with the rebuild because the junk yards are full of them you can get reasonably if you mess it up.||||reg||1
Z||000007||KRMFiero||06-23-2001||10:04 AM||||The Trans works, Just it has over 100K miles on it, it was in a huge car.... Figured i should rebuild it before it goes into the Fiero, maybe i'll just change the filter and stuff and rebuild it if i blow the tranny later<P>Kyle||||reg||1
Z||000008||Rodrv6||06-23-2001||10:33 AM||||A freind an I recently did the transmission in his Lumina APV.  It's the same basic transmission as the Fiero.  Make sure you have a lot of clean work area and a good manual.  If you take your time and do things in a very systematic way, it's not that difficult.  When I take one apart, I lay out the pieces on the bench in the order they came out.  That helps keep confusion down to a minimum.  Make sure you carefully match up the gaskets in the kit with the old ones.  You will discover you have more than you need in the kit.  The other thing to remember is to keep it CLEAN.  DO NOT use rags to wipe off parts as this will leave lint and that can cause problems.  Air drying is the best way.  You also have to be very careful with the seals in the clutch packs so that they don't get pinched or cut.<BR>I hope I haven't scared you off.  It's probably a little harder than rebuilding an engine, but it can be done by a "novice".  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Rod Schneider, Woodstock, Ga.  84 SE, 330,000 miles and counting, 88 GT, not nearly as many miles :)||||reg||1
Z||000009||SteveJ||06-23-2001||10:56 AM||||I wouldn't bother rebuilding it if it works. Those things go lots longer without problems.<P>I would say replace the vacuum module if it doesn't shift right. It's only $10 and the replacements are adjustable between soft and harsh shifts. The TV cable only controls the speed the shifts take place at.<P>Remember a 440T4 and converter uses about 2 gallons of fluid from a dry refill. Gotta pour in a gallon, start it and pour in more as it pumps the stuff around.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Mach10||06-23-2001||02:14 PM||||I've done a jag tranny (with some help) It looks scary, sounds scary, and at times *IS* scary, but it's not impossible. Do it slow, do it careful, and KEEP THE SERVICE MANUAL HANDY!!!!!!!!!!!!!...!<BR>||||reg||14
Z||000011||theogre||06-23-2001||08:58 PM||||This isn't a beginer project. At least this is a spare tranny so if it takes you 3 weeks, so what.<P>CLEAN CLEAN CLEAN. You must have a large clean area to work.<P>Work Slowly. Do Nopt throw out any parts until you are finished. <P>Compair every new part to the one it will replace. Some new parts may have small diferances. That is pretty common.<P>You absolutly Must Have the service book. They aren't rocket science but if you try it w/o the book the odds of having a working tranny when you finish get rather low. There are a bunch of things that are picky about how they go together. How some things go together isn't obvious and even a small error means a dead/unreliable tranny.<P>The other thing you really want are all the GM TSB's for that tranny. These tell you things that aren't in the manual. There are a bunch for each version of GM tranny.<P>To use TH125 as an example... there is a TSB that tells you where to look for a broken part of the input assembly that has a known problem and is prone to crack.||||reg||1
Z||000012||Mach10||06-23-2001||09:29 PM||||Ugh, I just remembered the Throttle body. Of all the bits, this is the pickiest. If you get ANY dirt/dust in it, the tranny will act weird or not work at all. That's the part I needed the most help with.||||reg||2
